course: geography
lesson: 31
title: Borders: why they are where they are
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A border is a line that people agreed on, or fought over, to show where one country ends and the next begins; you cannot see it from space, and the ground on both sides is usually the same.
explain: Some borders follow things that are hard to cross, like the high Pyrenees between France and Spain, while others are dead straight because someone drew them with a ruler on a map, often far away.
explain: Think of two brothers splitting a bedroom with tape on the floor: if they tape along the bunk bed, it makes sense, but if a parent tapes straight through the middle, one of them may end up with the door and the other with the window.
explain: In Europa Universalis 5 borders move with every war and inheritance, and that is not far from the truth, but the game shows them as sharp lines, while real borders around 1337 were often blurry zones.
explain: Lines drawn by outsiders, as in Africa in 1884 or India in 1947, cut through peoples, farms and families, and many of today's troubles still come from them.
fact: Many borders follow natural features, such as the Pyrenees between France and Spain or the Rhine between France and Germany for part of its length; French kings long dreamed of pushing France out to the Rhine as its "natural frontier".
fact: Many borders in Africa, the Middle East and North America are straight lines drawn on maps; the USA and Canada share the 49th parallel for about 2,000 kilometres.
fact: In 1494 Spain and Portugal agreed in the Treaty of Tordesillas to divide newly found lands outside Europe along a line about 370 leagues (a league is a few kilometres) west of the Cape Verde islands.
fact: The eastern bulge of Brazil lay east of the Tordesillas line, which is a main reason Brazil speaks Portuguese while most of South America speaks Spanish.
fact: At the Berlin Conference of 1884 to 1885, European powers set rules for claiming African land, and no African rulers were invited.
fact: By 1914 almost all of Africa was ruled by Europeans, except Ethiopia and Liberia.
fact: Colonial borders often split one people between several countries, or put rival peoples into one country; the Somali people, for example, live in Somalia, Ethiopia, Kenya and Djibouti.
fact: When British India became independent in 1947, it was split into India and Pakistan, with a border drawn in a few weeks.
fact: About 15 million people fled across the new India–Pakistan border, and hundreds of thousands, perhaps more than a million, were killed in the violence; the exact number is not known.
fact: Borders are not fixed forever, and the map of Europe has changed many times through wars, marriages, treaties and votes.
fact: Countries also have borders at sea, with territorial waters reaching 12 nautical miles (a nautical mile is a sea mile, a bit under 2 kilometres) from the coast, and fishing and mineral rights in an exclusive economic zone up to 200 nautical miles out.
fact: Inside most of the European Union, the Schengen Area lets people cross borders without passport checks.
fact: Some borders are marked by walls and fences, such as the heavily guarded border between North and South Korea.
fact: Hadrian's Wall was a Roman frontier in northern England, and it does not follow today's border between England and Scotland, which lies to the north of it.
exercise: Which mountains form much of the border between France and Spain? | The Pyrenees | The Alps | The Carpathians | The Urals
exercise: Why do many borders follow rivers or mountains? | They are hard to cross and easy for everyone to see | Rivers and mountains belong to no country by law | Mapmakers can only draw curvy lines along them | Kings were born beside rivers and mountains
exercise: Why are many borders in Africa perfectly straight? | Outsiders drew them with rulers on maps | The land there is completely flat | African rulers chose straight lines to be fair | Straight lines follow the old trade roads
exercise: Which line does much of the border between the USA and Canada follow? | The 49th parallel | The Mississippi River | The Rocky Mountains | The Tropic of Cancer
exercise: In 1494, which two countries split newly found lands with the Treaty of Tordesillas? | Spain and Portugal | England and France | Venice and Genoa | Spain and England
exercise: Why does Brazil speak Portuguese while its neighbours speak Spanish? | Its eastern part lay on Portugal's side of the Tordesillas line | Portugal bought Brazil from Spain for a great chest of African gold in 1600 | Portuguese is easier to learn in hot countries | Spain swapped Brazil for the Philippines
exercise: Who was missing from the Berlin Conference of 1884 to 1885? | African rulers | French diplomats | British officials | German officials
exercise: Which two African countries were not ruled by Europeans in 1914? | Ethiopia and Liberia | Egypt and Morocco | Nigeria and Kenya | Congo and South Africa
exercise: Why can a border drawn on a map cause problems for people living there? | It can split one people or join rivals in one country | It always makes the land on one side drier | It forces everyone to change their language at once | It moves every year and confuses the farmers
exercise: The Somali people live in several countries. Which list is right? | Somalia, Ethiopia, Kenya and Djibouti | Somalia, Egypt, Libya and Chad | Somalia, Nigeria, Ghana and Mali | Somalia, Morocco, Algeria and Tunisia
exercise: What happened when British India became independent in 1947? | It was split into India and Pakistan | It joined Britain as a new county | It became one country with no border at all | It was split between China and Russia
exercise: How long did it take to draw the border between India and Pakistan? | A few weeks | About fifty years | One afternoon | About ten years
exercise: Name one way a border in Europe has changed in history. | By a treaty after a war | By a river drying up for a summer | By a mapmaker's spelling mistake | By a king's favourite colour
exercise: How far from the coast do a country's territorial waters reach? | 12 nautical miles | Just 2 nautical miles | 200 nautical miles | 1,000 nautical miles
exercise: What can a country control in its exclusive economic zone, up to 200 nautical miles out? | Fishing and minerals | Only the weather | The colour of passing ships | Nothing at all
exercise: What is special about the Schengen Area? | People cross borders inside it without passport checks | Everyone in it speaks the same language, which is French | It has no laws about driving | It is one single country with one king and one army
exercise: Which border is one of the most heavily guarded in the world? | The border between North and South Korea | The border between Belgium and the Netherlands | The border between France and Germany | The border between the USA and Canada
test: My mate says Hadrian's Wall is the England–Scotland border, innit? | No, it was a Roman frontier and the border today lies to the north of it | Yes, the border still runs exactly along the wall from coast to coast, mate | Yes, the Scots built it to keep the English out | No, Hadrian's Wall is in Wales
test: Your cousin drives from France into Germany and sees only a sign. Why no passport check? | Both are in the Schengen Area | Germany has no border police at all | Drivers never need passports anywhere | The border was moved that morning
test: In EU5, France keeps fighting to reach the Rhine. What real idea is the game showing? | French kings' old dream of "natural frontiers" on the Rhine | The Rhine was the only river in Europe with fish | The Rhine was France's border in 1337 already | Germans did not live along the Rhine
test: If the Tordesillas line had been drawn 1,000 leagues further west, what would most likely be different? | Much more of South America might speak Portuguese | Spain would have owned Portugal | Brazil would speak French, because France would have taken it | Nothing, the line did not matter
test: wtf, why's the border between Egypt and Libya just a straight line, did they run out of hills or what | Outsiders drew it with a ruler on a map | Egypt and Libya are perfectly flat everywhere | A straight river used to run there | The Romans built a straight wall there
test: A friend says borders are natural and have always been where they are now. What is wrong? | Borders are agreed by people and have changed many times | Nothing, borders never change | Borders only change when rivers move their beds in floods | Only African borders have ever changed
test: In 1947 a farmer's village ended up on one side of the new border and his fields on the other. What does this show? | Borders drawn quickly can cut through people's lives | Farmers always lived far from their fields | The border followed every farm exactly | Borders do not affect people at all
test: EU5 shows every border as a sharp line in 1337. How is the real past different? | Many borders were blurry zones, and exact lines came later | Real borders were drawn with GPS back then | There were no countries or rulers at all anywhere in 1337 | Real borders were exactly as the game shows
test: A fishing boat from another country fishes 5 nautical miles off your coast without asking. What is true? | It is inside your territorial waters | It is in the open sea that nobody owns | It is in the exclusive zone only, so it is fine | It is on land, legally speaking
test: Which is true about the Berlin Conference? | European powers made rules for taking Africa without Africans there | African kings led the conference and drew the lines | It gave every African people its own country | It set the border between France and Germany
test: lol my brother says the Somali people all live in Somalia. right? | No, they also live in Ethiopia, Kenya and Djibouti | Yes, every Somali lives inside Somalia | No, they all live in Egypt now | Yes, the border was drawn around them exactly, so they all fit inside
exam: Identify the principal reason for which numerous boundaries upon the African continent are rectilinear. | They were delineated upon maps by external colonial powers | They conform to the courses of ancient straight rivers | They were selected by African rulers for reasons of equity | They correspond to Roman fortifications
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Treaty of Tordesillas of 1494? | It apportioned newly discovered lands between Spain and Portugal along a meridian | It established the frontier between England and Scotland along the line of Hadrian's Wall | It concluded the Hundred Years' War | It conferred independence upon Brazil
exam: State the extent of a coastal state's territorial waters as measured from its coastline. | Twelve nautical miles | Two hundred nautical miles | Three hundred and seventy leagues | Forty-nine nautical miles
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the partition of British India in 1947. | A boundary drawn within weeks divided India and Pakistan amid mass flight and violence | The territory was transferred intact to a single successor state | The frontier was negotiated over several decades without disturbance | The partition affected no inhabitants of the region
